NNAAMMEE
     mmtt, eejjeecctt — magnetic tape and removable media manipulating program

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmtt [--ff _d_e_v_i_c_e] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_c_o_u_n_t]
     eejjeecctt [--tt] _d_e_v_i_c_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmtt utility sends commands to a magnetic tape drive.  By default, mmtt
     performs the requested operation once.  Operations may be performed mul‐
     tiple times by specifying _c_o_u_n_t.  Note that _d_e_v_i_c_e must reference a raw
     (not block) tape device.  If _d_e_v_i_c_e is of the form “host:device” or
     “user@host:device”, mmtt writes to the named tape device on the remote host
     using rmt(8).  eejjeecctt is simply an alias for mmtt, with the oofffflliinnee command
     specified.  eejjeecctt may also be used to eject other types of removable me‐
     dia.

     The options for mmtt are as follows:

     --ff _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Operate on the _d_e_v_i_c_e specified.

     The options for eejjeecctt are as follows:

     --tt      Insert the device instead of ejecting.  For the cd(4) driver,
             this requests that the tray be closed.

     The available commands are listed below.  Only as many characters as are
     required to uniquely identify a command need be specified.

     eeooff, wweeooff  Write _c_o_u_n_t end-of-file marks at the current position on the
                tape.

     ffssff        Forward space _c_o_u_n_t files.

     ffssrr        Forward space _c_o_u_n_t records.

     bbssff        Back space _c_o_u_n_t files.

     bbssrr        Back space _c_o_u_n_t records.

     rreewwiinndd     Rewind the tape (_c_o_u_n_t is ignored).

     oofffflliinnee, rreewwooffffll
                Rewind the tape and place the tape unit off-line (_c_o_u_n_t is
                ignored).  On non-tape removable media, the oofffflliinnee command
                causes the media to be ejected when the last operation on it
                closes (i.e., the filesystem is unmounted).

     ssttaattuuss     Print status information about the tape unit.

     rreetteennssiioonn  Retension the tape (if this operation is supported by the tape
                unit).

     eerraassee      Erase the tape (if this operation is supported by the tape
                unit).

     eeoomm        Forward space to the end of the media.

     bblloocckkssiizzee  Set the tape blocksize to _c_o_u_n_t bytes.

     ddeennssiittyy    Set the tape density code to _c_o_u_n_t as specified in the SCSI2
                specification.

     If a tape name is not specified, and the environment variable TAPE does
     not exist, mmtt uses the device _/_d_e_v_/_r_s_t_0.  The TAPE variable is ignored by
     eejjeecctt.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     If the following environment variable exists, it is utilized by mmtt.

     TAPE    mmtt checks the TAPE environment variable if the argument _d_e_v_i_c_e is
             not given.

FFIILLEESS
     /dev/rst*                      raw SCSI tape interface
     /usr/src/sys/scsi/scsi_tape.h  list of SCSI2 density codes

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     mmtt returns a 0 exit status when the operation(s) were successful, 1 if
     the command was unrecognized, and 2 if an operation failed.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Eject the first CD device.  This will work even if there is no CD in the
     drive:

           $ eject /dev/rcd0c

